  "type": "singleCorrect",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Which one of the following statements is correct for electrolysis of brine solution?</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "zTUQ5",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is formed at cathode",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "OxYO9",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is formed at cathode",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "fJwWq",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is formed at anode",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "QRLDH",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>OH</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>is formed at cathode",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Brine solution is the concentrated solution of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaCl</mi></math>&nbsp;in water.</p><p>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaCl</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>aq</mi><mo>.</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><msubsup><mi>Na</mi><mtext>(aq)&#160;</mtext><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>Cl</mi><mtext>(aq)&#160;</mtext><mo>+</mo></msubsup></math></p><p>The cathode will attract the sodium ions from the sodium chloride and hydrogen ions from the water. The hydrogen ions gain 2 electrons and are reduced to form hydrogen molecules.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>aq</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>OH</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>aq</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p>The anode will attract the hydroxide ions from the water and chlorine ions from sodium chloride. The chlorine ions lose 2 electrons and get oxidised.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>Cl</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>OH</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>NaOH</mi></math></p>",
